  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1232 AM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Areas of frost will be possible Sunday morning.\n\nChilly high pressure over the Mid MS valley today will migrate into \nthe TN valley tonight. While not in the most favorable position to \nsupport strong radiational cooling, winds should still go calm after \nsunset as the pressure gradient relaxes, from NW wind gusts of 15-20 \nmph this afternoon to light WNW overnight. The NBM probabilities for \n36 degrees or lower range from 60-90 percent across the central and \nnorthern Piedmont into the northern Coastal Plain. This roughly \ncovers an area along and north of US-64, though excluding most of \nthe Triad and Triangle urban centers due to the urban heat island. \nRegardless, a Frost Advisory may be needed tonight into Sunday \nmorning over these areas as lows dip into the low to middle 30s, \nexcept upper 30s to around 40 in urban areas and the Sandhills to \nsouthern Coastal Plain. After collaborating with neighboring WFOs, \nwe decided to let the day shift take a closer look before issuing \nany products.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A dry stretch of weather well into the middle of \nnext week as temperatures gradually moderate. A pattern shift looks \nto take hold late next week, though the details are still uncertain.\n\nNorthwest flow at mid-levels this weekend will support near to \nslightly below normal temperatures in the low to middle 60s. \nGuidance shows quasi-zonal flow taking over early to middle of next \nweek, which should allow for moderating temperatures ranging from \nthe upper 60s to low 70s. This stretch of weather is going to be dry \nwith limited moisture return and a lack of systems tracking through \nthe region.\n\nThe period from Thursday into Saturday of next week is still highly \nuncertain, however is starting to indicate a prominent pattern \nshift. Much of the guidance is in decent agreement that as ridging \ndevelops across the western US mid to late week, this allows for \ndeep troughing to set up over the eastern US. The result is likely a \nContinental Polar airmass, which could bring our true taste of late \nfall. The 540-dm thickness line drops in some solutions as far south \nas GA and 850-mb temperatures drop below freezing, supportive of a \nwidespread freeze at some point next weekend. The also interesting \naspect to the pattern is that some ensemble cluster solutions, \nincluding some AI members, show an area of low pressure tracking \nfrom the TN valley to off the Mid-Atlantic coast Thursday into \nSaturday. Where that tracks is highly uncertain at this point, but \ncould bring rain to the region and possible light snow to the \nAppalachians.",
